A||||4||Mark||Valve Seal Replacement||||5||
Z||000000||Mark||11-22-2000||05:27 PM||||I'm in the process of changing my valve stem seals.  Although I do have compressed air and the tools needed to pressurize the cyl, I feel more comfortable using the "rope method".  That is, fish a nylon rope into the spark plug hole, then rotate the crank to compress the rope against the head and valves.  Only problem is the rope method doesn't work!  I've tried a little rope, alot of rope, thin rope, thick rope,... you get the idea.  Although the piston "tightens up" against the head, the valve still opens when I try to compress the spring.  Its like there are voids in the rope, and the voids are ALWAYS under the valves.   Is there something unusual about the shape of the V6 combustion chamber that would make this method unreliable?<P>Also, if I did use compressed air to hold the valves up, is there a reliable way that I could "lock" the crank with the piston at the top of the stroke.  This would at least limit how far the valves would drop if the air seal were inadvertently lost.||||reg||5
Z||000001||rockcrawl||11-22-2000||05:33 PM||||I have always used the compressed air method, and have never dropped a valve. If you put the piston of the cylinder your working on at the top of the bore, it will not be able to fall out completely. If it does fall, it can be retrieved with a thin magnet tool.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mark||11-22-2000||05:33 PM||||Just thought of a way to lock the engine with the piston at the top of the stroke.  I suppose I could put the car in high gear and apply the hand brake.<P>Any idea how far the valve could potentially drop with the piston at the top of the stroke?  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||11-23-2000||03:15 AM||||While playing around with a 2.8 engine from a Chevy Citation, I made the following observations:<P>If piston is at top dead center, the valve won't drop very far - you can grab the valve with your hand, and pull it back up easily.<P>If piston is at bottom dead center, valve can drop into the valve guide to where you can't reach it...  but not so far that it falls all the way past the guide and into the cylinder.  That's where rockcrawl's magnet trick comes in and saves the day  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. <P>As far as the "rope trick" goes, I have had luck using an old appliance cord in lieu of a rope.  It doesn't fray, and slips in the spark plug hole easily- might be less compressible, too.  Turning the engine so piston is (roughly) at BDC lets you feed the whole cord (2 feet?)in there.  <P>I remember having the cord squash somewhat, as you mention.  Think I had to compress the spring pretty far, then hit the retainer (using a block of wood as a drift) to knock it loose.<P>FWIW, I'm getting ready to do the same thing to my GT, so that should help jog my memory   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.      ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mark||11-23-2000||09:16 AM||||Hey guys,<BR>I used compressed air.  As a "safety" precaution, I put the piston at the top of the stroke, put the tranny in high gear, and set the hand brake before applying the air pressure to the cyl.  I had to tap the retainer lightly with a hammer to break it loose and allow the spring to be compressed.  I was then able to shut off the tankless air compressor to cut the noise.  With the piston at the top, the valve can only drop a small amount and is easily grabbed and pulled back up.||||reg||1
